<html>
<title>Disassembler</title>
<body>


<h1>Disassembler</h1>

<p>
All code shown in the <strong>Disassembled code</strong> panel (1) is disassembled in real time. Any part of memory can also be disassembled to an assembler file (.asm or .txt).
</p>

<p>
The disassembler interface is shown below:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_dis.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Disassembled code</strong> panel</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Dis</strong> button</li>
<li>(3) <strong>Start address</strong> text field</li>
<li>(4) <strong>End address</strong> text field</li>
<li>(5) Hex code not executed</li>
</ul>


<h2>Disassembling to file</h2>

<p>
Press the <strong>Dis</strong> button (2) to disassemble any part of memory to an assembler (.asm or .txt) file. The <strong>Start address</strong> (3) and <strong>End address</strong> (4) text boxes to the right of the <strong>Dis</strong> button define the range.
</p>

<p>
In the example above, pressing <strong>Dis</strong> will disassemble from address 0 to 100 (hexadecimal).
</p>


<h2>How disassembly works</h2>

<p>
Code is disassembled based on execution by the CPU. Therefore, only code that has been executed is marked as 180x code and will be disassembled. As shown by marker (5) in the example above, code at addresses 000D to 0012 (hexadecimal) has not been executed yet and is shown as data (hex code); when executed, these will become long branches (to 037D and 0258 (hexadecimal)).
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> Addresses 0015 and 0017 (hexadecimal) clearly contain data and will never be disassembled to 180x code. <code>BB</code> is used in the DIS instruction, and <code>F0</code> in the <code>OUT 3</code>.
</p>

<p>
The main advantage of execution-based disassembly is that data is shown as bytes instead of 180x code. For a complete disassembly, all code must first be executed. Parts of the code that are harder to reach can, however, be marked as code manually. Executed pseudo code will be shown as <code>DB</code> in the output file with the pseudo-code shown as comments.
</p>


<h2>Marking</h2>

<p>
For details on marking code, data, ASCII text, and address references, see the <a href="DirectMarking.htm">Memory Type Selection</a> section.
</p>


<h2>Output file</h2>

<p>
The output file is fully compatible with the A18 assembler and will include lists for:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Labels: showing all labels used in the disassembled code.</li>
<li>Unused or indirect labels: showing labels for which no branches or subroutine calls are found in the code. Most likely <code>SEP Rx</code> instructions exist in the code that cause jumps to these labels.</li>
<li>Out of range branches or addresses: showing which branches refer to addresses outside the disassembled code.</li>
</ul>
</body>
</html>
